];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Creatine Monohydrate",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-creatine-monohydrate/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best creatine monohydrate?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, Thorne Creatine: 5 g of micronized creatine monohydrate per scoop, nothing else in the tub, and NSF Certified for Sport, at about $0.49 per serving on Amazon when we checked (November 2026). Momentous is an equal alternative. If you are not drug-tested, Optimum Nutrition Micronized Creatine delivers the same 5 g dose for under half the price per serving, which is hard to beat on value."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Creapure better than regular creatine monohydrate?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Creapure is a German-made brand of creatine monohydrate with a long record of tight purity specifications. It is the same molecule as other monohydrate, and there is little evidence it works better. It gives you a known supply chain, but it describes the raw material, not the finished tub, so a sport certification such as NSF Certified for Sport tells you more about the product you actually buy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much creatine should I take per day?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The ISSN position stand supports 3 to 5 g of creatine monohydrate a day, every day. At that dose, muscle stores fill in about three to four weeks. A loading phase of about 20 g a day split into four doses for five to seven days fills them in a week, but it is optional and more likely to upset your stomach."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is micronized creatine better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Micronized creatine is regular monohydrate milled into finer particles, so it mixes into water more easily and leaves less grit. It is not absorbed better in any way the research has shown, and it does not change the dose you need. Most quality tubs are micronized now anyway, so treat it as a small convenience rather than a reason to pay more."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does creatine damage your kidneys?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In healthy adults, research at recommended doses has not shown kidney harm, and the ISSN considers creatine safe for healthy people. It does raise blood creatinine, which can make kidney lab results look worse, so tell your doctor before blood work. People with kidney disease or related conditions should get medical advice before taking it. This is not medical advice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do creatine gummies work?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Some do, but gummies have the worst track record in independent testing. NOW's 2024 tests found 5 of 12 brands had little to none of the creatine claimed, and a 2025 SuppCo round found four of six gummies failed while powders passed. Many gummies also hold only 1 to 1.5 g each, so reaching 5 g gets expensive. Powder is the safer bet."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I buy creatine separately or in my protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Separately, in most cases. A plain tub costs about $0.21 to $0.58 a day and lets you keep taking creatine on days you skip a shake. Protein blends with creatine are convenient but often cost more and sometimes underdose. Our guide to protein powders with creatine checks which blends deliver a full 3 to 5 g."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "By Goal",
    tags: [
      "best creatine monohydrate",
      "creatine monohydrate powder",
      "creapure creatine",
      "nsf certified creatine"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Purity-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Creatine Monohydrate</Fragment>
      Six plain creatine monohydrate powders sold on Amazon, ranked on independent certification, Creapure sourcing and what a 5 g dose really costs.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#quick-answer">The Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#how-we-ranked">How We Ranked</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#creapure-vs-generic">Creapure vs Generic</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing Explained</a>
      <a href="#micronized-and-forms">Forms and Gummies</a>
      <a href="#dosing">Dosing</a>
      <a href="#cost-math">Cost per Serving</a>
      <a href="#safety">Safety</a>
      <a href="#which-to-buy">Which to Buy</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The best creatine monohydrate for most people is Thorne Creatine: 5 g of micronized monohydrate per scoop, nothing else in the tub, and NSF Certified for Sport, at about $0.49 a serving on Amazon when we checked (November 2026). Klean Creatine adds Creapure to the same certification, and Optimum Nutrition Micronized Creatine delivers the identical dose for under half the price per serving if you are not drug-tested. Here is how the six compare on testing, sourcing and cost, plus the dosing and safety evidence behind them.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="quick-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Which Creatine Monohydrate to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Creatine monohydrate is the rare supplement where the cheap version and the expensive version contain the same molecule at the same dose. Every tub on this page delivers 5 g of creatine monohydrate per scoop, per the label at the time of writing. What you pay extra for is assurance: independent testing that the tub contains what it says and nothing it should not, a named raw-material source, and a powder fine enough to stir into water without a gritty sludge at the bottom of the glass.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people the best creatine monohydrate is Thorne Creatine. It is NSF Certified for Sport, micronized, contains nothing but creatine monohydrate, and costs about $0.49 per 5 g serving: about $44 for 90 servings on Amazon when we checked (November 2026); prices move. Momentous Creatine is essentially the same proposition at the same price. If you specifically want Creapure, the German-made raw material, Klean Athlete Klean Creatine pairs it with NSF certification, while Nutricost Creapure gives you the raw material for less but without a sport certification.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Creatine</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Dose / scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Testing</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx. cost / 5 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thorne Creatine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g micronized</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">NSF Certified for Sport</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.49</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Momentous Creatine Monohydrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">NSF Certified for Sport</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.48</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Klean Athlete Klean Creatine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g Creapure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">NSF Certified for Sport</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.58</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Optimum Nutrition Micronized Creatine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g micronized</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Banned-substance tested (per listing)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.23</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nutricost Creapure Creatine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g Creapure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brand says third-party tested</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.38</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nutricost Micronized Creatine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g micronized</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brand says third-party tested</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.21 (1 kg tub)</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are not drug-tested and just want the cheapest sensible option, Optimum Nutrition's unflavored Micronized Creatine and Nutricost's plain micronized powder both work out to less than half the price per serving of the certified tubs. The creatine works the same. The difference is how much you trust the brand's own quality control versus an outside certifier checking each lot.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide is about plain creatine on its own. If you would rather get creatine inside your protein shake, our guide to the <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-creatine/">best protein powder with creatine</a> checks which blends actually hit a 3-5 g dose, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-muscle-gain/">best protein powder for muscle gain</a> guide covers the protein to pair with a separate tub. For most lifters, a plain protein plus one of the tubs below is the cheaper and more flexible route.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-ranked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Ranked These Creatine Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run lab tests or a taste panel, and we do not claim to have used these products. The rankings are built from the brands' Supplement Facts panels and product pages, the NSF Certified for Sport listings the brands cite, the Amazon listings for each product, and the published research on creatine. Where sources disagreed, we went with the brand's current page and say so where it matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first test was simple: a single scoop had to deliver a full 5 g of creatine monohydrate with nothing else in the tub. That rules out flavored versions with sweeteners and colors (fine products, just not what this list is about), blends that dilute creatine with other ingredients, and alternative forms such as creatine HCl that cost more without better evidence. Unflavored monohydrate is the form used in nearly all of the research.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second test was purity assurance, and it carried the most weight. A product certified under NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport ranked above one that only says it is third-party tested, because certification means an outside body tests lots against the label and screens for banned substances, and the brand cannot simply assert it. A Creapure source earned credit too, but less, since Creapure describes the raw material, not the finished tub you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third came cost per 5 g serving, worked out from the Amazon price when we checked (November 2026) for the tub size listed. Creatine is cheap enough that the gap between the cheapest and dearest tub here is about 37 cents a day, or roughly $135 a year. That is real money, and it is the right lens for anyone who does not need a certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we checked that each product is sold on Amazon US under the name we use, since that is where most readers buy creatine. Buy from listings marked shipped and sold by Amazon or from the brand's own storefront, not an unknown third-party seller. One review of Klean Creatine noted Amazon buyers reporting broken seals, which is exactly the problem our <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">guide to buying protein powder on Amazon</a> explains how to avoid.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Creatine Monohydrate Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ranked first by independent certification, then by raw-material sourcing and cost per 5 g serving. Every pick delivers 5 g of creatine monohydrate per scoop with no other ingredients, per the label at the time of writing. Prices are what Amazon listed when we checked (November 2026) and move often; certifications can lapse, so confirm on the certifier's database before you buy.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="creapure-vs-generic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Creapure vs Generic Creatine: Does the Source Matter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Creapure is a brand of creatine monohydrate made by AlzChem in Germany. Supplement companies buy it as a raw material and put the Creapure logo on their tubs. Most other creatine on the market is made in China from the same basic chemistry. The pitch for Creapure is consistency: a single, long-established manufacturer with tight specifications for byproducts such as creatinine, dicyandiamide and dihydrotriazine that can form during production.
          </p>

          <p>
            Is that pitch worth paying for? Honestly, the evidence that generic creatine monohydrate from a reputable brand is meaningfully less pure is thin. Independent testing of creatine powders over the years has mostly found that plain monohydrate powders contain what they claim. The bigger quality failures in the creatine category have come from gummies and novel formats, not from plain powder in a tub. What Creapure buys you is a known supply chain, which some people reasonably value.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two things matter more than the logo. First, Creapure tells you about the raw material, but a finished product can still be contaminated or mislabeled after the raw material arrives at a factory. A sport certification such as NSF Certified for Sport tests the finished product. Second, sourcing changes. Momentous, which built its creatine around Creapure, now states on its own FAQ that it no longer sources from Creapure. Older reviews and Amazon listing titles may still say otherwise, so read the brand's current page before paying for a claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our view: if you are drug-tested, prioritize a sport certification first and treat Creapure as a bonus, which is why Klean Creatine ranks high. If you are not tested and like the idea of a known German source, Nutricost Creapure is a reasonable middle ground at about $0.38 per serving. If price is the priority, a plain micronized monohydrate from Optimum Nutrition or Nutricost is a perfectly defensible choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. NSF, Informed Sport and "Third-Party Tested" Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Supplements in the US are not approved by the FDA before they go on sale. The manufacturer is responsible for making sure a product is safe and labeled accurately, and enforcement mostly happens after problems surface. That is why independent certification matters more for supplements than it would for, say, a packaged food. It is the only way an outsider has checked the tub before you buy it.
          </p>

          <p>
            NSF Certified for Sport is the program most US professional and collegiate sports bodies point athletes toward. It includes label-claim testing, contaminant checks under the NSF/ANSI 173 standard, and screening for a long list of substances banned in sport, with lot testing and facility audits. Thorne, Momentous and Klean Creatine all carry it, per the brands. You can confirm any product by searching NSF's own Certified for Sport database, which is free and takes a minute.
          </p>

          <p>
            Informed Sport and Informed Choice are the equivalent programs run by LGC, more common on UK and European brands. Optimum Nutrition's creatine listings describe it as banned-substance tested, and some retailer pages tie that to Informed Choice, but we could not confirm a current certification for the US unflavored tub, so we do not rank it as certified. If you compete under drug testing, verify the exact product and lot on the certifier's site rather than trusting a listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is the phrase "third-party tested" on its own, which Nutricost uses. It means the brand says it sends products to an outside lab, but it does not tell you what was tested, against what limits, or whether results are published. That is not worthless, and it is common among budget brands, but it is a brand claim rather than a certification. For an untested recreational lifter, it is usually enough. For an athlete whose career could hinge on a positive test, it is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            If certification is the deciding factor across your whole supplement shelf, our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF-certified protein powder guide</a> lists certified proteins you can pair with a certified creatine, so the entire stack has been checked.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="micronized-and-forms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Micronized, Flavored, HCl and Gummies: What Form to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Micronized creatine is ordinary creatine monohydrate milled into smaller particles. It disperses more easily in water and is less likely to leave grit at the bottom of the glass. It is not a different form of creatine, and there is no good evidence it is absorbed better or works better. Most decent tubs are micronized now anyway, including Thorne, Optimum Nutrition and both Nutricost options here. It is a convenience feature, so do not pay much extra for the word on the label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flavored creatine is plain monohydrate plus acids, sweeteners, flavors and often colors. Nutricost alone sells a long list of flavors on Amazon. If a flavor helps you take creatine every day, that is a fair trade, but check the serving size: flavored tubs often hold fewer servings per gram because the flavoring takes up room, and one Nutricost flavored listing showed conflicting serving counts. Unflavored powder disappears into a protein shake, coffee or juice with little taste.
          </p>

          <p>
            Creatine HCl, buffered creatine, creatine ethyl ester and similar forms are marketed as needing smaller doses or causing less bloating. The International Society of Sports Nutrition's position stand concludes that monohydrate remains the most studied and effective form, and that the newer forms have not been shown to be superior. They usually cost more per day, so we left them out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gummies are the format to be most careful with. In early 2024 the supplement maker NOW tested 12 creatine gummy brands and reported that 5 had little to none of the creatine claimed, with signs of degradation into creatinine. A 2025 round by SuppCo of creatine products bought on Amazon found the powders met their label claims while four of six gummies failed. Some brands disputed the results and lab methods for gummies are tricky, but the pattern is consistent enough to matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gummies also have a plain dosing problem. Many hold around 1 to 1.5 g of creatine each, so reaching 5 g can mean four or more gummies a day, with the sugar or sugar alcohols that come with them, at several times the cost of powder. If you dislike powder, capsules are a more reliable alternative, though you will still swallow several to reach a full dose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Much Creatine to Take: The ISSN Dosing Guidance
          </h2>

          <p>
            The International Society of Sports Nutrition's 2017 position stand on creatine, led by Richard Kreider, is the most cited summary of the research. It describes creatine monohydrate as the most effective nutritional supplement currently available for increasing high-intensity exercise capacity and lean body mass during training. The effects are modest but real: more reps at a given weight, slightly better strength gains over a training block, and a bit more lean mass compared with training alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simplest protocol is 3 to 5 g a day, every day. At that dose, muscle creatine stores fill over roughly three to four weeks and then stay full as long as you keep taking it. A 5 g scoop from any tub on this page covers it. Timing matters far less than consistency, so take it whenever you will remember: with breakfast, in a post-workout shake, or stirred into coffee.
          </p>

          <p>
            Loading is optional. The classic loading phase is about 0.3 g per kilogram of body weight a day, roughly 20 g split into four 5 g doses, for five to seven days, then 3 to 5 g a day after that. It fills your stores within a week instead of a month. The trade-offs are more scoops, a higher chance of stomach upset, and a faster jump in water weight. Unless you have an event coming up, skipping the loading phase is perfectly reasonable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Larger people sometimes use the upper end of the range, and some research uses doses scaled to body weight, but there is little reason for most adults to go beyond 5 g a day for maintenance. More does not mean more muscle once your stores are full; the excess is cleared by the kidneys and leaves as creatinine. Vegetarians and vegans, whose diets contain little creatine, tend to start with lower stores and may notice a bigger effect, which is one reason it suits our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-vegetarians/">vegetarian protein</a> readers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Creatine is stable as a dry powder but slowly breaks down once dissolved, especially in warm or acidic liquids, so mix it shortly before you drink it rather than prepping a jug for the week. A level teaspoon of unflavored monohydrate is roughly 5 g if your tub arrives without a scoop, as some Optimum Nutrition tubs now do, though a cheap kitchen scale is more accurate.
          </p>
        </div>

