        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is Creatine Safe? Kidneys, Water Weight and Hair
          </h2>

          <p>
            Creatine monohydrate has one of the best safety records of any sports supplement. The ISSN position stand and its 2021 follow-up on common questions and misconceptions, led by Jose Antonio, conclude that short- and long-term use at recommended doses appears safe in healthy people, with studies running for months to years. That is not the same as risk-free for everyone, and this guide is not medical advice.
          </p>

          <p>
            The kidney question comes up most. In healthy adults, studies have not shown that creatine at normal doses harms kidney function. The catch is lab testing: creatine raises blood creatinine, the marker doctors use to estimate kidney function, so a routine blood test can look worse than your kidneys really are. Tell your doctor you take creatine before blood work. Anyone with existing kidney disease, diabetes affecting the kidneys, or who takes medication that affects the kidneys should talk to a doctor before starting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water weight is real but mostly misunderstood. Creatine draws water into muscle cells, and many people gain somewhere around 1 to 2 kg in the first weeks, more noticeably with a loading phase. That is water inside the muscle, not fat or puffy subcutaneous bloat, and it levels off. If you are making weight for a sport or tracking the scale closely during a diet, start creatine well before the weigh-in that matters, or skip loading.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hair loss rumors trace back to a single 2009 study in college rugby players that found a rise in DHT, a hormone linked to male-pattern baldness. It did not measure hair loss, and later research has not reproduced the finding. The 2021 ISSN review concluded the current evidence does not show creatine causes hair loss. If you are already worried about thinning, it is a fair question for a dermatologist, but it is not a reason most people need to avoid creatine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stomach upset is the most common real side effect, usually from large single doses during loading. Splitting the dose, taking it with food, or skipping loading solves it for most people. Pregnant or breastfeeding readers, teenagers and anyone managing a medical condition should get advice from a doctor or registered dietitian first, and our overview of <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">supplement safety</a> covers how to read labels and certifications more broadly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Creatine Monohydrate Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are a drug-tested athlete, buy an NSF Certified for Sport tub and confirm it on NSF's database. Thorne is the default, Momentous is an equal alternative when it is cheaper on the day, and Klean Creatine is the pick if you also want Creapure. The extra cost over a budget tub is about 25 to 37 cents a day, which is trivial next to a failed test.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are not tested and want the best value, buy Optimum Nutrition Micronized Creatine or Nutricost's plain micronized powder in the largest tub you will finish, and take 5 g a day. If you like the idea of a known German raw material without paying for certification, Nutricost Creapure is the middle option. MuscleTech Platinum Creatine is another widely sold budget tub at 5 g a scoop, though it lists no third-party certification, so it did not make our ranked list.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, the habit matters more than the brand. Creatine only works if your muscle stores stay full, so take it on rest days too. Put the tub somewhere you will see it, mix it into something you already drink daily, and give it a month before judging. If a shake is your daily habit, a <a href="/best-portable-blender-for-protein-shakes/">portable blender</a> makes it easy to mix creatine into protein at the gym, and our guide to <a href="/do-protein-supplements-build-muscle/">whether protein supplements build muscle</a> covers the other half of the equation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, skip anything that hides the dose. If a tub, gummy or pre-workout does not state a gram figure for creatine monohydrate on the Supplement Facts panel, assume it is not enough. Five grams of plain monohydrate from a brand you trust is all the research asks for, and every product above delivers it.
          </p>
