        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for a sourcing claim that is out of date</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Creatine sourcing changes. Momentous, long marketed as a Creapure product, now says on its own FAQ that it no longer sources from Creapure, while some listings and reviews still say otherwise. If Creapure matters to you, check the brand's current product page and the tub's label rather than an old review or an Amazon title written years ago.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating "third-party tested" as a certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A brand saying its creatine is third-party tested is not the same as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, where an outside body tests lots and publishes the listing. For recreational lifters the brand claim is usually fine. For drug-tested athletes, look the exact product up in the certifier's own database before buying, every time you switch brands.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Only taking creatine on training days</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Creatine works by keeping muscle stores saturated, which takes daily intake. Taking it only before workouts, or stopping on rest days, means your stores fill more slowly and drift down. Take 3 to 5 g every day at whatever time is easiest to remember, and judge the results after a month of consistent use, not a week.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying gummies for convenience</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Creatine gummies are where most quality failures have turned up. NOW's 2024 testing found 5 of 12 gummy brands had little to none of the creatine claimed, and many gummies hold only 1 to 1.5 g each, so a full dose means several a day at several times the price of powder. Plain powder or capsules are more reliable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Panicking over the scale or a blood test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Expect around 1 to 2 kg of water weight in the first weeks, mostly inside muscle; it is not fat. Creatine also raises blood creatinine, which can make a routine kidney test look worse than it is. Tell your doctor you take creatine before blood work rather than quietly stopping and wondering what changed.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

